{
  "requirement": "`matches` holds the results found. `complete` is true when the search exhausted the scope. Reaching the exact limit is compatible with a complete result if no further match exists.\n\n```rust,ignore\n#[non_exhaustive]\npub struct SearchResults<T> {\n    pub matches: Vec<T>,\n    pub complete: bool,\n}\n\nimpl<T> SearchResults<T> {\n    pub fn new(matches: Vec<T>, complete: bool) -> Self;\n}\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The tests adequately cover `requirements.sdk.graph.search.SearchResults`.\n\n**Obligations derived from the text**\n1. The type has public `matches` and `complete` fields, is generic over `T`, and has a `new(matches, complete)` constructor. It is `#[non_exhaustive]`, so callers outside the crate must use `new`.\n2. `matches` holds the results found.\n3. `complete` is true when the search exhausted the scope.\n4. Reaching the exact limit is still complete when no further match exists.\n\n**Coverage**\n- **Shape, constructor, field values.** `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/search.rs#fn[callers_can_construct_complete_and_partial_search_results]` is an integration test, so it sits outside the crate. It builds `SearchResults::new(vec![found], false)` and `SearchResults::new(vec![found], true)`, once with `TextMatch` and once with `StructuralMatch`. It asserts `complete` is false and true respectively, and it asserts the contents of `matches`. That shows `new` is usable externally, the type works for both element types, and the two fields keep what was passed in.\n- **`complete` semantics.** `crates/trust-but-verify/src/sources/tests.rs#fn[completeness_is_false_only_when_a_match_is_omitted_and_errors_propagate]` runs `search_text` over a fixture with two matches. It asserts the following (`limit`, match count, `complete`):\n\n| `limit` | matches | `complete` |\n|---|---|---|\n| `None` | 2 | true |\n| `Some(2)` | 2 | true (exact limit, nothing further) |\n| `Some(1)` | 1 | false |\n| `Some(0)` | 0 | false |\n\n  It also asserts that a limit of `Some(0)` with no matching content gives an empty result with `complete` true. Together these cover the exact-limit case and the \"omitted match\" case.\n- **Public export.** `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]` checks that the type appears in the documented export inventory.\n\n**Residual points**\n- `#[non_exhaustive]` is not tested directly, since a compile-fail test would be needed. It is a declarative attribute with no conditional or transformation of its own. The external-crate use of `new` in the integration test is consistent with it.\n- Structural-search completeness is not exercised here. This requirement's text states completeness semantics generally, and the search behaviour is tested by the provider requirements the same test cites.\n\nI found no gap.",
